WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.951
How does your class get to school?
Ask them all, and you have data.

2
00:00:09.171 --> 00:00:13.862
Today you will record it, draw it,
sort it, and say how likely it is.

3
00:00:14.362 --> 00:00:19.423
First decide what would answer it. Some
answers are names, some are numbers.

4
00:00:19.643 --> 00:00:25.495
Walk, bus, car or bike are groups
you can name. Categorical data.

5
00:00:25.715 --> 00:00:30.127
How many brothers and sisters? Those
are numbers you count. Discrete data.

6
00:00:30.347 --> 00:00:37.034
Twenty-two children answer, one stroke each.
Every fifth stroke crosses four, like a gate.

7
00:00:37.254 --> 00:00:43.245
Now count in fives. Nine walk, six by bus,
four by car, three by bike. Twenty-two.

8
00:00:43.745 --> 00:00:48.760
The same twenty-two answers, drawn three
ways. Each is good at a different job.

9
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:55.018
A bar chart gives each group a bar against
a scale. Walking is tallest, at nine.

10
00:00:55.238 --> 00:01:01.739
A pictogram uses a key. One circle stands
for two children, so nine is four and a half.

11
00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:07.207
A dot plot puts one dot for each child,
so you can count them one by one.

12
00:01:07.707 --> 00:01:14.116
A Venn diagram asks two questions at
once. Is it even? Is it more than ten?

13
00:01:14.336 --> 00:01:20.001
Twelve is even and more than ten. Both
answers are yes, so it goes in the overlap.

14
00:01:20.221 --> 00:01:25.283
Eight is even, but not more than ten.
Fifteen is more than ten, but not even.

15
00:01:25.503 --> 00:01:32.051
Seven is neither, so it sits outside both
hoops. A Carroll diagram gives it its own box.

16
00:01:32.551 --> 00:01:38.124
Class 4A and class 4B answered the same
question. Both have twenty-two children.

17
00:01:38.344 --> 00:01:43.081
In 4A walking wins, with nine.
In 4B the bus wins, with eleven.

18
00:01:43.301 --> 00:01:47.713
Four children come by car in each
class. That is the same in both.

19
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:52.902
The same total does not mean the
same answers. Look at every group.

20
00:01:53.402 --> 00:01:57.953
Some things cannot happen. Some
are sure to. Most sit in between.

21
00:01:58.173 --> 00:02:01.934
A dice has no seven, so
rolling one is impossible.

22
00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:05.637
But every face is less than
seven, so that is certain.

23
00:02:05.857 --> 00:02:11.152
A bag holds nine red counters and one
blue. Red is likely, blue is unlikely.

24
00:02:11.372 --> 00:02:15.087
Tossing a coin is maybe. It might
land on heads, and it might not.

25
00:02:15.587 --> 00:02:21.020
This spinner has four equal parts, so
each should come up a quarter of the time.

26
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.627
Ten spins are lumpy. One part came up five
times, when it should be two and a half.

27
00:02:26.847 --> 00:02:32.327
Now spin it a thousand times. Each part
lands close to two hundred and fifty.

28
00:02:32.547 --> 00:02:37.330
A few spins tell you little. Many spins
show what the spinner really does.

29
00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.639
You can plan what to collect,
and tally it as the answers come.

30
00:02:41.859 --> 00:02:46.642
You can draw it three ways, sort it
two ways, and compare two classes.

31
00:02:46.862 --> 00:02:51.970
And you can say if something is impossible,
maybe, likely or certain. Well done.
